    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my materials class and im a bit confused. why do we actually need to put steel rebar inside concrete for foundations? i get that concrete is strong, but whats the deal with the relationship between its compressive strength and the tensile strength of the steel? like why do they need each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Great question! Concrete is excellent at handling compression, but it's brittle under tension—it cracks and fails when pulled apart. Steel rebar, on the other hand, is ductile and has high tensile strength, so it takes over the pulling forces that concrete can't handle. Together, they form a composite where concrete resists squeezing and steel resists stretching, which is exactly what a foundation needs when soil shifts or loads bend the structure. Plus, the two materials have similar thermal expansion coefficients, so they expand and contract together without breaking their bond.",
      "source": "target",
