    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so basically the steel is there to catch the cracks before they become a huge problem. does that mean the rebar is basically working \"inside\" the concrete to hold it together from within? like is there a specific way the bond between the two works to make sure they don't just slide past each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Exactly—you've got the right mental image! The rebar is like a skeleton inside the concrete, holding it together from within. But the bond between them isn't just friction; it's a combination of mechanical interlocking and chemical adhesion. The ribs or deformations on the rebar's surface physically bite into the surrounding concrete, creating a strong grip that prevents sliding. Plus, the concrete shrinks slightly as it cures, which clamps down on the rebar like a tight hug—this adds even more friction to keep them locked together. That's why you can't just use smooth bars; the deformations are crucial for transferring stress from the concrete to the steel.",
      "source": "target",
